      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14262816/assets/f0d4e8ddf3a1b6cacab75f3207575695de5790f8223e30bc948e93daf37c939f.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to one decimal place, find the area of $△ABC$ shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}BC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={30}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x-1}{x+2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})(x+2) & =x-1 \\\\ x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})+2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =x-1 \\\\ x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})-x & =-1-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})-1) & =-1-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})) & =1+2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1+2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})}{1-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})} \\\\ x & =5.098\\,0…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =x-1=5.098\\,0-1=4.098\\,0…, \\\\ AB & =x+2=5.098\\,0+2=7.098\\,0….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}(4.098\\,0…)(7.098\\,0…) \\\\  & =14.543\\,8… \\\\  & ≈14.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the one decimal place.",
